                <pb facs="0333" n="245" sameAs="frus:frus1933v04/pg245"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.3415/2760: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dearing</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-01-09T11:00:00-05:00">January 9, 1933—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 4:32 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4. Department’s circular January 7, 3 p.m. Embassy’s telegram 3, January 4, 7
                    p.m. Peru’s position is that set out in circular. No replies yet received from
                    Argentina, Brazil and Chile. Believed at Foreign Office changes going against
                    Bolivia which would willingly accept any suggestion promising cessation
                    hostilities.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dearing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
